<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 8 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Fall of Rome</h1><p class="path-tagline">How the western half of the ancient world's greatest state came apart.</p><p class="path-intro">Rome did not fall in a day, and half of it did not fall at all. This path traces the long unravelling of the western empire: the reforms that bought time, the frontier that broke at Adrianople, the sacks of the eternal city, and the quiet afternoon in 476 when a barbarian general sent the imperial regalia east. It ends with the Gothic kings who kept the lights on in Italy and the eastern empire that carried Rome onward for a thousand years.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/roman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 27 BCE – 476 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Roman Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Begin with the whole story: the power the fifth century inherited and squandered.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · r. 284–305 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Diocletian</span><span class="path-step-why">The reformer who split the empire to save it, a century before the end.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 378 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Adrianople</span><span class="path-step-why">378 CE: the Goths destroyed an emperor and his army, and the frontier never truly closed again.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/alaric-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaric-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · d. 410 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Alaric I</span><span class="path-step-why">The Gothic king whose army took Rome in 410, the first sack in eight centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/attila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 406–453</span><span class="path-step-name">Attila</span><span class="path-step-why">The Hun who bled both empires and showed how little stood behind the walls.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odoacer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 433–493 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Odoacer</span><span class="path-step-why">The soldier who deposed the last western emperor in 476 and ruled Italy as king.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 454–526</span><span class="path-step-name">Theodoric the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">The Ostrogoth who governed Italy in Roman style: the fall, seen from the inside, looked like continuity.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/byzantine-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-arab-siege-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 330 CE – 1453 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Byzantine Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The half that did not fall: Rome's eastern empire endured to 1453.</span></span></a></li></ol>
